# .dockerignore — shrinks the build context and keeps secrets out of it.
#
# The build context is everything the daemon ships to the build process. Even
# a multi-stage Dockerfile that only COPYs go.mod, go.sum, and the source tree
# still streams the whole context to the daemon. Excluding noise here speeds
# up builds and prevents secret-bearing files from ever entering the build
# pipeline. This is defence in depth — .dockerignore is not an authorization
# boundary; secrets must never be checked in.

# Version-control state and editor noise.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo

# Build outputs that must never land in the image. The builder stage produces
# its own binary inside the container; a host-built one would defeat the
# reproducibility -trimpath / -ldflags guarantee.
bin/
dist/
yalla
yalla.exe
yalla-api
yalla-api.exe
yalla-worker
yalla-worker.exe
*.test
*.exe

# Test caches and coverage reports.
coverage.txt
coverage.out
*.coverprofile

# OS junk.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# npm wrapper runtime artefacts (downloaded at install time, not source).
node_modules/

# Local-only operator artefacts and credentials. Even though these should
# never exist in a clean checkout, exclude them explicitly so a developer who
# experiments locally cannot accidentally ship them.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
secrets/
.codex-cache/

# Repo-tracked documentation and tooling that are not part of the runtime
# image. The builder stage's `go build ./cmd/yalla-api` does not need any of
# these.
docs/
ralph/
outputs/
Codex.dmg

# The Dockerfile and .dockerignore themselves do not need to enter the build
# context — but excluding them would break `docker build .` invocations that
# resolve them by relative path. Keep them in.
